# The Loud Terminal Is the Unintelligible One

**A communications-integrity standard for MWAA terminals — argued as safety engineering, delivered through the Design Manual.**

*Run: quiet-by-design-standards-for-mwaa-terminals-2 · Remediated final draft (Stage 3, post-gate) · 2026-08-11*

---

## Executive Summary

MWAA holds approximately $6.99 billion of committed capital at IAD and $2.39 billion at DCA under the fifteen-year Airline Use and Lease Agreement effective January 1, 2025.[^1] On July 29, 2026, the President, the U.S. Transportation Secretary, United's CEO, and MWAA jointly announced a Dulles transformation described at more than $20 billion, provisional pending Board action.[^2] Every square foot of that work passes through the seven-volume MWAA Design Manual.[^3]

The recommendation is narrow: add a tiered communications-integrity standard to the Design Manual this capital cycle, issued by the Office of Engineering under delegated standards-setting authority, taken to the Board as an information item at the next Business Administration Committee, and escalated to a resolution only if AAAC consultation surfaces material airline objection or an AUL cost-center reclassification. After the January 29, 2025 midair collision and the February 2026 NTSB final report, the standard is defensible only as a floor that raises the probability the announcement that matters — the gate change, the shelter-in-place, the IROPS diversion — reaches the passenger who needs it.[^4] NFPA 72 Chapter 24 requires a Speech Transmission Index of at least 0.45 at 90% of measurement locations in each Acoustically Distinguishable Space, average 0.50.[^5] ACRP Research Report 175 targets STI 0.50 with 10–15 dB(A) signal-to-noise;[^6] IEC 60268-16:2020 supplies the measurement method.[^7]

Design-time acoustic treatment runs roughly two to four times below retrofit at commercial-benchmark midpoints, based on non-airport indicative pricing.[^8] The Design Manual binds consultants and contractors today; its reach into tenant audio is the subject of the amendment, contingent on verification of the 2025 AUL's majority-in-interest provisions.[^9] Debt-service coverage compresses from 1.63× in 2024 to roughly 1.3× through 2029 as approximately $5.5 billion of new debt phases in,[^10] which is why the amendment must be scoped to work still in programming, not programs already in design. The Board question is not whether to fund a program. It is whether to write the paragraph.

