## The Loud Terminal Is the Unintelligible One

Trade-press summaries of a Johns Hopkins ICU observation put alarm rates at 771 per bed per day.[^11] The Joint Commission's response was National Patient Safety Goal NPSG.06.01.01, phased in 2014 and 2016: every accredited hospital had to inventory its alarms, rank them, govern them, and prove it.[^12] Indiscriminate broadcast was itself the safety failure.

The analogous condition in an airport holdroom is well documented. IAD moved 29.01 million passengers in 2025, 10.53 million of them international — the fastest 2025 growth among the fifty largest U.S. airports.[^13] DCA saw a year-over-year decline in 2025 following the January 29 collision[^4] and the 43-day federal shutdown. Every one of those passengers expected the announcement that mattered to reach them.

MWAA is about to specify, from the studs up, an enormous run of new terminal space. The July 29 announcement covers more than 5 million square feet: Concourses C and D replaced, AeroTrain extended, mobile lounges retired, the Saarinen Main Terminal preserved.[^2] The committed AUL figures are firm — roughly $6.99 billion at IAD and $2.39 billion at DCA, the latter including the eventual replacement of the Terminal 1 main concourse.[^1] The anchor tenant has noticed the price. Cranky Flier described United CEO Scott Kirby as visibly uncomfortable and cited an airline briefing pegging fully loaded IAD transformation cost per enplanement at $90.64, against $12.88 today.[^14] A standard that lives in the Design Manual survives program shrinkage, re-scoping, or a change of administration. A case built on program durability does not.

After NTSB AIR-26/02, no board member wants a policy paraphrased as "airport cuts safety announcements."[^4] That instinct is correct — and it is the argument. NFPA 72 Chapter 24 sets an intelligibility floor because a terminal is not one acoustic environment.[^5] You fail that floor by being loud, reverberant, and cross-broadcast: precisely the condition an unspecified terminal defaults to. High ambient noise pushes STI down the qualitative bands of IEC 60268-16 — toward "Poor" and "Bad" at the frequencies that matter most for emergency announcements.[^7] The louder the terminal, the less intelligible the announcement that counts.

Other airports' reduced-announcement models are policy analogues, not design precedents. San Francisco International reports its Quiet Airport program removed roughly 90 minutes of daily announcement time in its International Terminal and reduced paging by roughly 40% — SFO's own figures, without published measurement methodology.[^15] The same record holds documented passenger complaints and missed-flight anecdotes.[^16] Announcement reduction without intelligibility engineering is subtraction, not design.

The accessibility floor is non-negotiable. 28 CFR 35.160 obligates MWAA to ensure effective communication with people with disabilities.[^17] The 2010 ADA Standards §219 and §706 require assistive-listening systems in each assembly area where audible communication is integral to use.[^18] ACRP Report 239 treats digital-first substitution as partial, not sufficient, for travelers with disabilities and older adults.[^19] A regime that reduces broadcast without mandating redundant visual, digital, staffed, and hearing-loop channels compounds exclusion. One planning note: the Saarinen Main Terminal was determined National Register-eligible in 1978, and Section 106 consultation with the Virginia SHPO and ACHP runs six to eighteen months, longer with a Memorandum of Agreement[^20] — reason to keep the amendment's first applications outside the eligible-district envelope.

Governance is the technology. Automated announcement platforms can be specified in Design Manual terms: centralized management, replay and audit logs of every broadcast, elimination of manual-initiation errors, templated multi-language delivery.[^21] Enforcement matters as much as the platform: annual acoustic inspections keyed to lease renewal, 30/60/90-day cure notices, lease-management escalation only for pattern noncompliance.

The financial case rests on rework avoidance, not new capital. Commercial industry pricing provides two directional benchmarks: new-construction acoustic ceiling treatment at approximately $5–$15 per square foot and retrofit acoustic panel treatment at approximately $18–$45 — non-airport, medium-confidence indicative figures. Design-time treatment runs roughly two to four times below retrofit at the midpoints.[^8] An order-of-magnitude range for the full stack of $12–$40 million — ceiling premium, FIDS redundancy, mobile notification platform — sits between 0.17% and 0.57% of the committed IAD program.[^8] The window is narrow: debt-service coverage compresses from 1.63× in 2024 to roughly 1.3× through 2029 as some $5.5 billion of new debt phases in.[^10]

The strongest counter-case is redundancy: NFPA 72, ADA, and ACRP 175 already set the floors that matter. Half-right. NFPA 72 Chapter 24 binds emergency voice; it does not require ADS mapping as a general design deliverable, govern routine PA, or compel governance of manual-initiation error. ACRP 175 is guidance, not code. The amendment's marginal content — the ADS map at each design milestone, representative-traffic commissioning, audit-logged governance, channel-redundancy floors, an IROPS override, and a tenant-audio boundary tied to the AUL — is exactly what the existing floors do not require.

United operates about 50 jetbridge gates and moves about 67% of IAD traffic; American holds roughly 53% of DCA market share — both directional trade-press figures, not primary MWAA rate-setting disclosures.[^22] A standard that cannot reach tenant audio governs half the soundscape. The amendment, once the 2025 AUL's majority-in-interest provisions are confirmed, should be written to reach it.[^9] Exercise it first where friction is lowest: the DCA Terminal 1 concourse replacement or, if that project has passed scope-definition, the earliest IAD C/D element still in programming. Either way, Concourse E — opening this fall with fourteen new United gates[^23] — carries the first commissioned STI and ambient baseline under representative traffic. That baseline becomes the number every future MWAA facility is measured against.

Return to the ICU. The Joint Commission did not tell hospitals to reduce alarms. It told them to prove which ones mattered, that those would be heard, and that the machinery for governing the rest was on the record. A decade later the ICUs are audible in the right places, at the right times, to the right people. That is what a communications-integrity standard buys an airport. The Board question is not whether to fund a program. It is whether to write the paragraph.

