## Airport Decision Card

**Owner and instrument.** MWAA President and CEO sponsors. Vice President for Engineering drafts and issues the amendment under the delegated standards-setting authority exercised for annual Design Manual revisions. Board receives it as an information item at the next Business Administration Committee (target: November 2026 cycle). A formal resolution is initiated only if AAAC consultation surfaces material airline objection or the 2025 AUL cost-center analysis requires MII treatment.

**External framing convention.** Internal engineering name: "quiet-by-design." External policy name in all Board papers, press releases, DOT correspondence, and airline briefings: "MWAA Communications-Integrity Standard." MWAA Communications drafts and enforces the convention.

**Airline-consultation lane (parallel to drafting, on the critical path).**
- Weeks 1–3: confidential briefings to United and American executive station managers and capital counsels.
- Week 4: formal AAAC briefing; thirty-day comment window opens.
- Week 12: written MWAA position on cost-recovery categorization keyed to specific 2025 AUL cost centers. No amendment language enters any scope document until the Week 12 position letter is on the record.

**First 180 days.**
- **Days 1–30 — verification.** Current acoustic, PA-zoning, and STI content of the Design Manual; DCA Terminal 1 project phase; 2025 AUL MII and consultation text; NFPA 72 edition in force at each AHJ; Section 106 posture with Virginia SHPO and ACHP for any Saarinen-adjacent scope.[^20]
- **Days 30–90 — drafting and stakeholder.** Mandatory-tier language keyed to NFPA 72 Ch. 24,[^5] ACRP 175,[^6] IEC 60268-16:2020,[^7] 28 CFR 35.160,[^17] and 29 CFR 1910.95.[^24] Airline-consultation lane; Fire Marshal briefings at each AHJ.
- **Days 90–180 — application and adoption.** Pilot to DCA Terminal 1 or earliest eligible IAD C/D element in programming. Board information item; publish the amendment in the annual Design Manual revision. Commission the first ADS map and baseline STI/ambient reading at Concourse E within 90 days of opening.[^23]

**Cost and funding.** Capital stack $12M–$40M one-time (analyst construction from non-airport commercial pricing benchmarks; see footnote 8), allocated roughly 60/40 IAD/DCA. Funding candidates: program contingency inside the committed AUL work; PFC amendment for the mobile notification platform; General Airport Revenue Bonds only if a stand-alone capital element requires it. Operating tail approximately $0.8M–$2.5M/year across both airports.

**Dependencies.** 2025 AUL MII and cost-recovery text; Section 106 consultation on Saarinen-adjacent application, 6–18 months;[^20] FAA Part 139 emergency-communications compliance baseline;[^25] Fire Marshal AHJ concurrence at each airport; represented-workforce bargaining posture on any new duties.

**Leading indicators.**
1. Amendment language published in the next Design Manual annual revision and cited by section in the first eligible major terminal-package scope documents.
2. Commissioned STI and ambient readings at Concourse E under representative traffic within 90 days of opening, benchmarked against ACRP 175 and NFPA 72.
3. Missed-boarding, service-recovery, and ADA-effective-communication complaint rates at amendment-governed facilities, benchmarked against non-amendment facilities.

**Failure modes / stop conditions.**
- If the current Design Manual already contains equivalent or stronger acoustic and PA-zoning content, the recommendation contracts to a governance-and-verification amendment.
- If the 2025 AUL MII text materially constrains tenant-audio enforcement, the tenant tier drops from mandatory to recommended pending renegotiation.
- If the Concourse E baseline shows existing MWAA design already meets ACRP 175 and NFPA 72 targets under representative traffic, mandatory application contracts to governance and IROPS behavior.

**What would change this recommendation.**
- A measured MWAA STI/ambient baseline showing existing spaces already meet targets under representative traffic.
- Publication of peer-airport primary measurement methodology permitting direct-copy operating patterns.
- FAA publication of Part 139 amendments touching PA and communications, moving requirements outside MWAA's discretion.

